      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "## Read me first\n\n**SageAttention comes from the launch flag, not from a node.**\n\n    python main.py --use-sage-attention\n\n`wrap_attn` then passes SageAttention to the override as `func`\n(`comfy/ldm/modules/attention.py:193`), so it becomes what `SolAttnH3` falls\nback to and what recomputes the sink's query rows. Without the flag you get\nPyTorch SDPA, roughly 2.5x slower at these shapes.\n\n**Do not add `Patch Sage Attention KJ` to this graph.** It assigns\n`model_options[\"transformer_options\"][\"optimized_attention_override\"]` — the\nsame key `SolAttnH3` uses. They overwrite each other, the last node in the\nchain wins, and the loser does nothing at all: no warning, no log line, just\nthe speedup quietly gone.\n\n**Confirm it is running.** Once per run the console prints:\n\n    [sol-attn-h3] SM120, CuTe DSL available, backend=cute_sm120, DiT blocks: 50\n    [sol-attn-h3] correctness gate PASS ...\n    [sol-attn-h3] routing density {... 'effective_density': 0.25 ...}\n\n`backend=triton` on an SM89+ card means the CuTe runtime is missing.\n`sparse_calls: 0` means every call declined — the reason is in `declined`.\n\n**Frame length** snaps up to the model's 17k+5 grid: 124 (~5 s), 141, 158, …,\n362 (~15 s). Off-grid values are rounded up silently, so 125 gives you 141.\n\n### This graph ships at 362 frames on purpose\n\nMeasured on an RTX 5080, real i2v with a first frame, NVFP4 weights, 8 steps:\n\n| frames | node off | node on |\n|---|---|---|\n| 362 | 150.2 s | **137.1 s — 1.10x** |\n| 124 | 35.2 s | 39.0 s — **0.90x, the node costs you 10%** |\n\nThe sink is recomputed densely on every sparse call, and a first frame makes it\n1436 rows. At 124 frames that is 8.7% of the sequence and the node cannot earn\nit back; at 362 it is 4.9% while attention has grown quadratically.\n\nIf you drop to 124 to iterate, either set `enabled` to false or switch\n`sink_mode` to `text` (34.2 s, 1.03x). `text` stops keeping the reference-image\nrows exact, which is what holds identity — switch back for the final render.\n"
      ],
      "widgets_values_named": {
        "text": "## Read me first\n\n**SageAttention comes from the launch flag, not from a node.**\n\n    python main.py --use-sage-attention\n\n`wrap_attn` then passes SageAttention to the override as `func`\n(`comfy/ldm/modules/attention.py:193`), so it becomes what `SolAttnH3` falls\nback to and what recomputes the sink's query rows. Without the flag you get\nPyTorch SDPA, roughly 2.5x slower at these shapes.\n\n**Do not add `Patch Sage Attention KJ` to this graph.** It assigns\n`model_options[\"transformer_options\"][\"optimized_attention_override\"]` — the\nsame key `SolAttnH3` uses. They overwrite each other, the last node in the\nchain wins, and the loser does nothing at all: no warning, no log line, just\nthe speedup quietly gone.\n\n**Confirm it is running.** Once per run the console prints:\n\n    [sol-attn-h3] SM120, CuTe DSL available, backend=cute_sm120, DiT blocks: 50\n    [sol-attn-h3] correctness gate PASS ...\n    [sol-attn-h3] routing density {... 'effective_density': 0.25 ...}\n\n`backend=triton` on an SM89+ card means the CuTe runtime is missing.\n`sparse_calls: 0` means every call declined — the reason is in `declined`.\n\n**Frame length** snaps up to the model's 17k+5 grid: 124 (~5 s), 141, 158, …,\n362 (~15 s). Off-grid values are rounded up silently, so 125 gives you 141.\n\n### This graph ships at 362 frames on purpose\n\nMeasured on an RTX 5080, real i2v with a first frame, NVFP4 weights, 8 steps:\n\n| frames | node off | node on |\n|---|---|---|\n| 362 | 150.2 s | **137.1 s — 1.10x** |\n| 124 | 35.2 s | 39.0 s — **0.90x, the node costs you 10%** |\n\nThe sink is recomputed densely on every sparse call, and a first frame makes it\n1436 rows. At 124 frames that is 8.7% of the sequence and the node cannot earn\nit back; at 362 it is 4.9% while attention has grown quadratically.\n\nIf you drop to 124 to iterate, either set `enabled` to false or switch\n`sink_mode` to `text` (34.2 s, 1.03x). `text` stops keeping the reference-image\nrows exact, which is what holds identity — switch back for the final render.\n"
      },
      "color": "#222",
      "bgcolor": "#000"
    }
